## Doctrine 12

**An instruction in a subagent prompt is not a control.** *(W-28 retro,
2026-08-05.)* Four audit lenses were each given an explicit, emphatic output
constraint, stated near the top of the prompt as a hard requirement: the
output is destined for a **public** repo, so never emit a literal credential
path, a contributor's name, a hostname, or a scheduler job identifier —
describe mechanisms generically. **Three of the four violated it**, returning
an absolute production path, a real contributor name pulled from git history,
the live database filename, job identifiers, and third-party vendor names.
Nothing reached the committed artifact — but only because the orchestrator
redacted every finding by hand while transcribing and scanned the result
afterwards. Had the orchestrator been less careful, or the artifact longer,
it ships. Measured compliance on an explicitly-flagged hard requirement: **1
of 4**. Prompt text sets a tendency, not a bound. **Anything load-bearing for
safety, disclosure, or irreversibility needs a mechanical step between the
agent's output and the world** — a scan, a denylist, a gate — and that step
is the deliverable, not the sentence in the prompt. This is the same reason
review agents get a read-only-git *constraint* rather than a polite request.
Filed as collabb#60 for W-29 to design against.

## Doctrine 17

**Do not delegate a fact that a file on disk can answer.** *(W-30 retro,
2026-08-06.)* A load-bearing design decision rested on the exact YAML key
that restricts a subagent's tool grant. An agent was dispatched to verify it;
the web docs were behind the domain allowlist, so it answered from memory —
"comma-separated string, not a YAML list" — confidently, and wrongly. It is
an array. The correct answer was sitting in a local plugin skill file the
whole time.
Delegating a lookup adds a fabrication surface and buys nothing when the
source is local: the subagent has no more access to ground truth than you do,
and less context about why the answer matters. This is doctrine 7's F-16
amendment ("the judge is not exempt") generalised past judges — **any agent
asked to verify one fact fabricates at the same rate as the agent that
asserted it, while arriving dressed in the authority of having been asked to
check.** Delegate breadth (survey many files, find where a thing lives).
Read the file yourself for the one fact a decision turns on.

## Doctrine 25

**A subagent told to be read-only wrote to a real repository, and nothing
stopped it.** *(W-18 retro, 2026-08-09.)* A design-panel agent was instructed,
in its prompt, to run only read-only git commands and never to mutate the
working tree. A failed `cd` in its shell caused several commands to execute
against the live nested `www` checkout instead of its scratch directory; it
created a real merge commit on the PO's branch, then "repaired" it with a
hard reset. It self-reported, the orchestrator verified the checkout was
intact (HEAD, branch, all refs, untracked files), and no push occurred — so
the cost this time was zero.

This is doctrine 12 with a fresh instance and a sharper edge: the constraint
was not a disclosure rule but a *write* rule, the agent was not careless in
intent, and the repo's own guardrails did not deny the git write because the
path was inside the project tree. **Read-only is a property of the tool
grant, not of the prompt.** Where a subagent has no reason to write, give it
an agent type whose grant excludes writes, point it at a throwaway copy, and
never at a directory holding the PO's in-flight work. Filed as a backlog item
rather than fixed here, because the fix is a guardrail/agent-definition
change that a session is not permitted to make.

## Doctrine 64

**A prohibition expressed as a filename pattern cannot hold when an
ordinary entry point NESTS the thing you banned.** *(W-96 retro,
2026-08-18.)* Doctrine 58 says never background an in-place mutation
harness and never edit its targets while it runs, and W-119 measured 2 of 3
review lenses running one despite an explicit instruction.

W-96 tried to fix that with a sharper instruction: every lens prompt named
the harnesses by filename, banned `*-mutations.sh` outright, and said
compliance would be verified afterwards. It also said, in the same prompt,
that running `backends/github-issues/tests/run.sh` was "fine and
encouraged."

**`run.sh` invokes two in-place mutation harnesses** — `prio-hygiene-
mutations.sh` and `w24-config-mutations.sh`. So the instruction forbade a
thing and simultaneously encouraged the wrapper that does it. A lens
obeyed both literally, and a second lens then observed the tracked config
template sitting dirty with a `__W24_MUTATED__` artifact mid-review and
correctly reported it. Nothing was harmed, but only because those harnesses
restore in an EXIT trap — that is their discipline, not this session's.

Two things generalise. **A ban on running X must be a ban on running
anything that runs X**, which means enumerating callers, not filenames —
the same losing game doctrine 31 describes for write verbs, and the same
answer: control the resource, do not pattern-match the invocation. And the
verification step is what saved it: checksums of every mutable target
captured before the fleet and compared after (`IDENTICAL`), which is a
mechanical check between the agents' output and the tree, exactly what
doctrine 12 asks for. **Keep the check even when the instruction gets
better** — the instruction got better here and was still wrong.
